{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by davide familiare and david fincher, based on the book the founding fathers of facebook: the inside story of the creation of facebook and the personalities who made it big. the film stars jesse eisenberg as mark Zuckerberg, a brilliant but reclusive college student who creates the social network website.\n\nthe film begins with mark's arrival at harvard university, where he bonds with his roommates and classmate edgar (armie hammer) and danny (doom patel), as well as his fellow student and future business partner, ari (andy garcia). mark's life takes a dramatic turn when he creates facebook as a social networking site for harvard students.\n\nas facebook gains popularity, mark's relationships with his friends and acquaintances begin to fray. he becomes increasingly isolated and paranoid, driven by a desire to prove himself as a brilliant programmer and entrepreneur. the film explores the tension between mark's desire for isolation and his growing need for connection.\n\nthe film also delves into the complexities of mark's relationships with his friends and family. his on-again, off-again romance with rachel (kirsten dunst) is portrayed as a tumultuous and often toxic relationship. his friendship with edgar, who becomes a rival and adversary, is also explored in depth.\n\nthe film features a stellar supporting cast, including josh peck as naomi's boyfriend, and cassie nayef in a minor but memorable role. the cinematography is stunning, capturing the gritty and often bleak atmosphere of harvard's campus.\n\nthe film's direction is masterful, with fincher and familiare coaxing outstanding performances from the cast. the editing is sharp and precise, moving the film's narrative forward at a breakneck pace.\n\nhowever, some critics have accused the film of perpetuating a sanitized and overly idealized portrayal of mark's life. the film glosses over the more problematic aspects of mark's personality and behavior, such as his treatment of women and his obsessive focus on facebook.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of success, friendship, and the human need for connection. while it may not be a completely accurate portrayal of mark's life, it is a compelling and deeply human story that lingers long after the credits roll.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.20552276074886322}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.00035589560866355896}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.04680097848176956}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0009609610424377024}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.5923129320144653}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.2819807529449463}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.016800811514258385}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -1.847726889536716e-05}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.0005004345439374447}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -9.393251093570143e-05}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.09783100336790085}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0009151084814220667}, {"token": " dav", "logprob": -3.2621054649353027}, {"token": "ide", "logprob": -1.8776681423187256}, {"token": " fam", "logprob": -6.976188659667969}, {"token": "ili", "logprob": -1.997288465499878}, {"token": "are", "logprob": -0.200214222073555}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.1362553834915161}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.1336657702922821}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -5.936446541454643e-05}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -0.019399086013436317}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.2576214075088501}, {"token": " based", "logprob": -1.3763792514801025}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0023350848350